a historic referendum in Ireland today  could relax that country's strict  anti-abortion laws Jonathan Vigliotti is  in Dublin where Irish citizens from all  over the world have arrived to vote  Jonathan good morning good morning and  we've been watching people filing in all  morning long to cast their vote this is  expected to be an incredibly tight vote  we'll learn the results tomorrow morning  ultimately those I've spoken with say  this is a referendum on the future  direction of the nation for a country so  closely entwined with the Catholic  Church months of polarizing protests  have signaled a seismic cultural shift  voters today will select yes or no to  repeal Ireland's 8th amendment which  bans all abortions except when a  mother's life is at risk the law was  created in 1983 supported by those who  feared Ireland having its own roe v wade  moment since the 1973 ruling legalized  abortion in America Ireland has been  considered a pro-life beacon it's a  torch genomic brain wants to carry for  unborn children with disabilities grace  was born pink and screaming her  three-year-old daughter Grace has Down  syndrome  it wasn't my rice determinates to decide  when to terminate her life but  pro-choice campaigners like Ivana bachik  say the current law ultimately hurts  women just as caused this immense trauma  has blocked any reform of abortion law  and of course it hasn't stopped Irish  women having abortion in 2016 over 3,000  women from Ireland travelled to the UK  to have abortions  that's almost nine a day and it's  estimated that 1,500 illegal abortion  pills are ordered online each year the  Catholic Church in Ireland has  encouraged its followers to vote no to  abortion but critics here say its moral  authority has weakened after a series of  child abuse scandals and this is a  nation that has been leaning more  liberal in the past few decades in 1995  they legalized divorce in 2015 they  legalized gay marriage if abortion is  legalized women will be allowed to  terminate within the first twelve weeks  of pregnancy  Kayla Jonathan Vigliotti reporting from  Dublin thank you
 

